<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Archimedes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 287–212 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Archimedes of Syracuse</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Archimedes of Syracuse was the greatest mathematician and engineer of the ancient Greek world. He calculated areas and volumes of curved figures, approximated pi, founded statics and hydrostatics with the principles of the lever and buoyancy, and is credited with the Archimedean screw. During the Roman siege of Syracuse his machines defended the city, and he was killed when it fell in 212 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/archimedes.jpg" alt="Archimedes" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Domenico Fetti · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Domenico-Fetti_Archimedes_1620.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Hellenistic and Roman periods, Greek was the language of science and scholarship: Euclid’s Elements of geometry, the works of <b>Archimedes</b>, the medical writings of Hippocrates’ school and of Galen, the astronomy of Ptolemy, and the critical scholarship of the Library of Alexandria, which edited and preserved the classics.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-greek">Ancient Greek · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Humanists printed <b>Archimedes</b>, Euclid and Ptolemy in Greek, and the recovery of rival ancient schools, such as the atomism of Lucretius, showed that the ancients had disagreed among themselves; voyagers found lands, peoples, plants and animals unknown to the ancients; and printing put accurate drawings, maps and tables…</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Hieron II, Rome's ally from 263 BCE, Syracuse enjoyed peace and <b>Archimedes</b> worked there.</blockquote><a href="/state/syracuse">Syracuse</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Syracuse, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Syracuse, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.08,15.28&amp;year=-239" aria-label="Syracuse on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4933 828 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Archimedes"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5383 1098h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5383 1098h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.08,15.28&amp;year=-239">What was here in 239 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;There are some, King Gelon, who think that the number of the sand is infinite in multitude…&#8221;</p><footer>The Sand-Reckoner &#183; tr. T. L. Heath, 1920 &#183; from T. L. Heath, Archimedes (1920) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/35550/pg35550-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Give me a place to stand on, and I can move the earth…&#8221;</p><footer>saying reported by Pappus of Alexandria &#183; tr. T. L. Heath, 1920 &#183; the saying as later writers report it; from T. L. Heath, Archimedes (1920) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/35550/pg35550-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I apprehend that some, either of my contemporaries or of my successors, will, by means of the method when once established, be able to discover other theorems in addition, which have not occurred to me.&#8221;</p><footer>The Method, letter to Eratosthenes &#183; tr. T. L. Heath, 1920 &#183; from T. L. Heath, Archimedes (1920) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/35550/pg35550-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
